  "abstract": "<jats:p>This paper provides theoretical and empirical comparisons\nof three recent hierarchical plan repair algorithms: SHOPFIXER, IPYHOPPER, and REWRITE. Our theoretical results\nshow that the three algorithms correspond to three different\ndefinitions of the plan repair problem, leading to differences\nin the algorithms’ search spaces, the repair problems they can\nsolve, and the kinds of repairs they can make. Understanding\nthese distinctions is important when choosing a repair method\nfor any given application.\n\nBuilding on the theoretical results, we evaluate the algorithms\nempirically in a series of benchmark planning problems. Our\nempirical results provide more detailed insight into the run-\ntime repair performance of these systems and the coverage of\nthe repair problems solved, based on algorithmic properties\nsuch as replanning, chronological backtracking, and back-\njumping over plan trees.</jats:p>",
